# Updates, repairs and maintenance

Name the software or device affected, the action that is available and what will happen if the person takes it. Keep maintenance apart from an urgent hazard in the home. A notification count leads to maintenance detail. It does not show that a problem has been fixed.

<h2 id="scope-and-native-ownership">What Home Assistant owns</h2>

An update entity is On when a newer version is available. It is Off when the installed version is the latest or when the latest version was skipped. It can also be unavailable or unknown. It reports the installed version, the latest version and, if one was skipped, the skipped version, with a release summary and a release link. Whether it can install, and whether it reports progress, depends on the integration. Home Assistant Repairs supplies the details or steps for each issue. civica changes how a badge looks, not the state of a repair. See the [Home Assistant update documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/update/), which opens outside this site.

This is reference specification HA-GAP-020. The application must also meet the integration acceptance checks below. The examples are simulated and do not show that a feature or adapter is installed.

<h2 id="states-and-content-decisions">What to show for each condition</h2>

| Condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Update available | The installed version, the offered version and the release information. | Guess how serious it is from the version number. |
| Skipped release | The skipped version, when it is supplied. | Say the latest version is installed. |
| Downloading or installing | Only the stages and progress the provider supplies. | Add a made-up percentage or promise that it can be cancelled. |
| Restart needed | What will be affected and how long it is expected to stop, if known. | Restart when someone opens the card. |
| Repair dismissed or unresolved | That it was acknowledged, kept apart from whether it was fixed. | Treat removing a badge as proof the issue is resolved. |

<h2 id="integration-contract">Rules for the integration</h2>

1. Use the native permissions and the native update and repair flow. Whether an update can be installed, backed up or cancelled depends on the integration.
2. Show the software's actual title as well as any household name for the device. Keep release identifiers exactly as supplied, and say when a release-note link leaves Home Assistant.
3. Before installing, state the known interruptions and what is needed first, as the provider gives them. Do not invent how long it will take, and do not assume every device can roll back.
4. Home Assistant owns the numbers in attention counts. The [notification badge](../components/notification-badge.html) does not create, send, dismiss or resolve maintenance records.

<h2 id="accessible-interaction">Keyboard and screen reader use</h2>

- Put release details before the main action in a readable form. Compare versions in text, not only with coloured tags.
- Announce a change of stage once. Keep focus and the native repair dialog's name when the theme changes.
- Make failed and skipped states visible without colour and without a hover tooltip.

<h2 id="failure-and-recovery">When something goes wrong</h2>

- If an install is rejected, keep the installed version and the provider's explanation. Never change the version before Home Assistant reports it.
- If progress stops being reported, say progress is unavailable and check the native operation before trying again.
- If a repair only gives instructions, show that route. Do not add a “Fix now” button that does not exist.

